WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:04.800
It has been dubbed hell on earth&nbsp;
for those incarcerated here.&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.320
Like when you are like dead, you&nbsp;
cannot eat. You cannot drink.&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.480
A place commonly known for its&nbsp;
ubiquitous torture practices.&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.800
Indefinite detention without charges&nbsp;
and trial is a human rights violation.&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:31.760
Lack of medical care is a human rights violation.
Guantanamo is turning 20 years old - 39 out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.520
almost 800 prisoners have been kept there through&nbsp;
today. Some of them without criminal charges.&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.720
We’re ashamed that everything that&nbsp;
made this country one that we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:57.840
say that was a free country, that had equal&nbsp;
justice for all, has abandoned all of that.&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.400
We are here at the infamous Guantanamo&nbsp;
Bay detention facility in Cuba and I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.520
be taking you with me to look at the world’s&nbsp;
most notorious prison. Guantanamo first opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.800
20 years ago and some of its remaining inmates&nbsp;
have been waiting for their trials ever since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.800
often in inhumane conditions. In this video, I'll&nbsp;
take you through why Guantanamo still exists, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:35.120
examine whether it can ever really be shut down.&nbsp;
But first let’s look at how everything began.&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.840
On September 11, 2001 the United States was hit&nbsp;
by unparalleled terror attacks carried out by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:54.240
al-Qaida. Thousands of people died. The United&nbsp;
States and its allies were shaken to the core.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.640
The US government was looking for an&nbsp;
answer that was equally forceful and clear.&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.680
Make no mistake: The United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:13.360
will hunt down and punish those&nbsp;
responsible, for these cowardly acts.&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:21.680
The answer came soon: The United States and its&nbsp;
allies launched a global war on terror. More and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.560
more suspects were captured – and later sent&nbsp;
to the US navy base at Guantanamo Bay in Cuba.&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.800
A place outside the public eye. Far from&nbsp;
American soil. Where the US constitution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.400
does not exist and nobody is watching.
Very soon, Guantanamo hit the headlines:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.360
reports of torture came to light. And that led&nbsp;
to some fundamental, and unsettling, questions:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.920
Was America, a longtime fighter for human&nbsp;
rights, hiding its terror suspects in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.360
detention facility, tucked away on a small bit&nbsp;
of land in Cuba, so that nobody could see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.840
was really happening here, behind barbed wire&nbsp;
fences? Was America losing its moral authority?&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.040
The base is only accessible by plane from the&nbsp;
United States. Only the US military can allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:12.800
visitors to the base. We have to play by their&nbsp;
rules. And that means: filming is very limited.&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.960
Guantanamo consists of a&nbsp;
navy base, a military court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:24.560
and the infamous prison – that part, however,&nbsp;
is off limits for our camera – for good reason.&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.560
Can you summarize from the perspective&nbsp;
of Amnesty International, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.280
are the human rights violations&nbsp;
that are taking place in Guantanamo?&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.680
Indefinite detention without charges&nbsp;
and trial is a human rights violation.&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:45.280
Lack of medical care is a human rights violation.
Lack of treatment for torture is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:46.677
human rights violation.
Lack of accountability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.677 --> 00:03:49.280
for torture is a human rights violation.
There are so many human rights violations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:58.960
involved in Guantanamo. That’s why we’ve opposed&nbsp;
it for so many years and that’s why we think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.760
Biden administration really needs to take&nbsp;
every measure it can to shut it down now.&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.120
The biggest obstacle for us is that everything&nbsp;
you see in this video has been recorded under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.360
surveillance by the US Army. It has&nbsp;
gone through extensive censorship. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.520
it is archive footage that was given to us&nbsp;
and therefore shows a sugarcoated version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.400
of Guantanamo. No picture or video may leave&nbsp;
the base without the Army’s approval. Plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.880
we didn’t get any access to the detention&nbsp;
facilities, so it’s hard to find out what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.880
really going on behind these walls. But one man&nbsp;
who has spent years as an inmate can tell us more.&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:40.560
Mohamedou Ould Slahi, a former resident of&nbsp;
Germany, was held in Guantanamo for 14 years. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.560
was suspected as being the head recruiter for the&nbsp;
9/11 attacks but was never charged with a crime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.520
His book “Guantanamo Diaries” – now hitting the&nbsp;
big screen as “The Mauritanian” - shows in great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:57.680
detail how he was brutally tortured. He suffers&nbsp;
from post-traumatic stress disorder to this day.&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:07.520
Like when you are like dead, you cannot eat.&nbsp;
You cannot drink. You cannot do anything and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.080
you cannot sleep most more. You cannot&nbsp;
sleep. And you just like waiting to die.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:19.760
No, this is like the worst manifestation I had.
I wake up sometimes just like three or four nights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:27.200
ago, I woke up and I was shaking so scared because&nbsp;
I saw my jacket on the door and I thought it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:32.800
someone coming to get me. And it took me a very&nbsp;
long time. Sometimes I wake up, I cannot breathe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:40.320
you know, but I'm facing this, you know, because&nbsp;
like, getting your ducks in a row is a myth.&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.800
Nancy Hollander defended Mohamedou at court.&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:51.840
Well, Mohammed was tortured more&nbsp;
severely than anyone else in Guantanamo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:59.600
with the possible exception of a prisoner named&nbsp;
Katana, who is still there. (…) But you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:05.840
if you see the movie (…) you'll find&nbsp;
out that what the movie says and shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:13.440
is what happened to him. (…) You know, the movie&nbsp;
can't show 70 days of not being given more than an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:20.320
hour to sleep, night on, some nights no sleep,&nbsp;
so that he basically began to hallucinate.&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.320
At the heart of Guantanamo is the military&nbsp;
court – the so called Camp Justice.&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.960
The majority of the remaining inmates are&nbsp;
still waiting for their trials - some of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:40.240
20 years after being brought here. Among&nbsp;
them are the alleged masterminds of 9/11&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:47.360
like Khalid Sheikh Mohammed and Ramzi Binalshib.
Some 13 detainees are cleared to be released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.440
but are still being kept in Guantanamo&nbsp;
– without any charges at all.&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:07:00.080
We cannot film at court. But we can talk to&nbsp;
defense attorney Anthony Natale. His client&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.720
is the alleged al-Qaida terrorist&nbsp;
Al-Nashiri who arrived here in 2006&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:10.000
and has been waiting for his trial ever since.
Guantanamo has been seen internationally as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.960
a stain on American history. So where&nbsp;
does this place put the United States?&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:18.880
We’re ashamed that everything&nbsp;
that made this country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:26.400
one that we could say that was a free country,&nbsp;
that had equal justice for all, has abandoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.720
all of that. And that’s sad and I don’t&nbsp;
know how we’re going to be able to recover.&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:40.480
It has become a rallying point to recruit&nbsp;
other extremists. It has been something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:46.160
has been thrown in the face of Americans&nbsp;
and the US government whenever we want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:54.880
criticize human rights anywhere else. I don’t&nbsp;
see it. To be quite frank: if there was a reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.640
they’re not telling us and they’re&nbsp;
not telling the public of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.880
why do they have to continue this.
In the shadow of the detention camp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:12.880
lies another side of Guantanamo. The US Naval&nbsp;
Base has been there for almost 120 years.&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:22.320
Residential areas resemble small-town America.&nbsp;
This is where soldiers and their families live.&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:25.840
Cuba’s only McDonald’s is here.&nbsp;

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.840
The contrasts to the detention&nbsp;
camp couldn’t be clearer.&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.600
This is what the military wants us&nbsp;
to show: some normalcy. A Navy base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:42.800
where people go about their everyday lives. To us&nbsp;
it really seems like a world of double standards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:47.360
You have the notorious prison and the court&nbsp;
on the one hand, and the 6,000 inhabitants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.800
of Guantanamo on the other. But the prison and&nbsp;
what happens there is a forbidden topic for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.440
and we’re not even allowed to&nbsp;
talk to people on the streets.&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.880
But we are allowed to film at Radio GTMO, the&nbsp;
oldest radio station of the armed forces network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.840
The soundtrack for Guantanamo comes&nbsp;
from here and she is Gitmo’s voice.&nbsp;

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:15.680
Hello Gitmo, hello hello, welcome to your&nbsp;
morning show with DJ Candis otherwise known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:21.440
as Petty Officer Annaliss Candelaria.&nbsp;
It’s pretty early in the morning, 8 a.m.&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.040
Court trials and the prison&nbsp;
are not part of their coverage.&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:32.080
It’s not really part of the culture here.&nbsp;
That’s another side of the base. Here on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.800
this side we have a whole different type of&nbsp;
operations going here, because as you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:44.240
this is an important fueling station. This is&nbsp;
an important base for the military in general.&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:49.680
The new pride of the base is the school&nbsp;
which was just reopened in a new building.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:54.480
This elementary school classroom comes with&nbsp;
state-of-the-art technology and allows its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:00.880
students somewhat of a normal childhood. Five&nbsp;
kilometers from the notorious detention facility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:03.840
But prison and court are not on the curriculum.&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:11.760
We focus on the idea of curriculum standards,&nbsp;
our mission is to provide the best education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:18.640
that out students should get.
For several US presidents the detention camp was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:24.080
a thorny topic on their agenda. President Obama&nbsp;
was the first who wanted to close Guantanamo.&nbsp;

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.200
I don’t want to pass this problem on to&nbsp;
the next president no matter who it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:34.800
Are we going to let this linger on for&nbsp;
another 15 years? For another 20 years?&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:44.720
Obama failed - President Trump changed course.
To re-examine our military detention policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:50.080
And to keep open the detention&nbsp;
facilities at Guantanamo bay.&nbsp;

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:54.880
And the Biden administration&nbsp;
has again shifted back.&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:00.720
Yes our goal is to close Guantanamo. I don’t&nbsp;
have a timeline for you but there’s a process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:06.480
there are different layers of the process.
But how long that will take is incredibly unclear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:10.880
Ultimately, the detention center was built&nbsp;
to detain terrorists and war criminals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:17.280
and have them face trial in a military court.&nbsp;
Out of 800 detainees that were held in Gitmo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.520
only eight have been convicted and four&nbsp;
of these convictions have been overturned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:27.840
That leaves us with the question: what is taking&nbsp;
so long? And can there ever be an end to this?&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.040
Well, there is a law on the books&nbsp;
in the United States that says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:45.840
no one who has ever been in Guantanamo can come&nbsp;
to the US for any purpose, trial, medical etc.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:53.040
But moving them, just making a Guantanamo North is&nbsp;
not the answer to this question. These people need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.040 --> 00:12:04.160
to be released. We can't hold people for 20 years&nbsp;
without any charge because, according to the US,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:09.840
there's not enough evidence to charge them,&nbsp;
but we somehow know they're dangerous.&nbsp;

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:15.520
Cliff Sloan was President Obama’s envoy&nbsp;
for closing Guantanamo. He believes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:18.160
Biden administration is running out of time.&nbsp;

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:26.640
There are many things that could be done and&nbsp;
that need to be done by President Biden and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:31.360
the administration, and I don't doubt their&nbsp;
commitment and their good faith. But the pact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:38.800
needs to be picked up to know. The 27 who are not&nbsp;
charged with anything need to be transferred to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:45.120
other countries right away, and especially with&nbsp;
13 who are approved for transfer. In the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:51.360
year of the Biden administration. We've only&nbsp;
seen one person transferred from Guantanamo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:57.760
and that is far, far too slow of a pace.
Do you think Guantanamo will ever close down?&nbsp;

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:04.320
Yes, yes, absolutely. I think even the US&nbsp;
government will come to realize that having an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:11.840
entire prison camp on their military base in Cuba&nbsp;
to hold a handful of men were right now 39. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:18.880
as that number get smaller and smaller&nbsp;
it will become more and more clear and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:25.840
how irrational it is, how absurd it is.
Can it? Absolutely! Will it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:33.840
God only knows. But it certainly can be&nbsp;
closed. All the needs can be accommodated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:41.680
without having a prison that is&nbsp;
riddled with a history of torture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:50.400
A court system which is flawed beyond repair.
It’s a shameful anniversary.&nbsp;

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:57.360
The Anniversary Guantanamo is a stain&nbsp;
on the United States, but as a result.&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:07.280
The answer isn't. Either turn the other&nbsp;
way or to raise our hands in frustration.&nbsp;

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:15.040
The answer (is to?) move forward and close&nbsp;
it and end this long, unfortunate chapter.&nbsp;

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:19.600
As promptly as (missing text) And I hope&nbsp;
that this year, the 20th anniversary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:24.800
is going to be the year that we are finally,&nbsp;
finally, going to see Guantanamo closed.&nbsp;

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:30.320
You know, I thought at the beginning of the&nbsp;
Obama administration that it was going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:36.320
happen. It clearly didn't I thought at the&nbsp;
beginning of the Biden administration that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:44.480
it was going to happen, and now I don't see how,&nbsp;
unless he really moves quickly and he is willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:53.200
to expend the political capital necessary to do&nbsp;
that. But you know it all comes down to politics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.080 --> 00:15:06.000
How far is he willing to go, given the Republicans&nbsp;
who refused to move on any issue whatsoever?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:12.800
And I just don't know. Now I'm beginning to be&nbsp;
more pessimistic than I was at the beginning.&nbsp;

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:18.000
The debate about the closure of the Guantanamo is&nbsp;
as old as the detention camp itself. Presidents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:24.640
famously vowed to transfer inmates to the mainland&nbsp;
US – and famously failed. Twenty years after 9/11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:30.080
the detention camp at Guantanamo Bay continues&nbsp;
to be a stain on America's global reputation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:39.840
And several dozen detainees are&nbsp;
still waiting for their trials.

